{"title":"2011 IMO Problems/Problem 4","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/d/f3d1431ad346632b25a0e7b3f12ebe19723b3960.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" /> be an integer. We are given a balance and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> weights of weight <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/9/9390c1c52ddf39c4dbc880ef4219d6d18eeebc6b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^0,2^1, \\cdots ,2^{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"117\" height=\"18\" />.  We are to place each of\tthe <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> weights on the balance, one after another, in such a way that the right pan is never heavier than the left pan.  At each step we choose one of the weights that has not yet been placed on the balance, and place it on either the left pan or the right pan, until all of the weights have been placed.\nDetermine the number of ways in which this can be done.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Call our answer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/8/7987ffa8a557c433381a8250fa991b77298ce1b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" />.\nWe proceed to prove <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/3/e83d5915d38060763106bdb1083cb72d966f231e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W(n)=(2n-1)!!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"143\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>It is evident <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/8/898e0f40b05be50c7c52c33ea32ddbad60fd47a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W(1)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Now, the key observation is that smaller weights can <i>never</i> add up to the weight of a larger weight, <i>ie</i> which side is heavier is determined completely by the heaviest weight currently placed. It follows, therefore, that the number of ways to place <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> weights on the balance according to the rule is the same no matter which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> distinct powers of two are the weights, as each weight completely overpowers any smaller weight and is completely overpowered by any larger weight. That is, there is the 1st heaviest weight, the 2nd heaviest, the 3rd, ..., the n-th heaviest, and each weight is negligible compared to any heavier weight. Thus, any valid placement of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> weights of weight <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/9/9390c1c52ddf39c4dbc880ef4219d6d18eeebc6b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^0,2^1, \\cdots ,2^{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"117\" height=\"18\" /> can changed by replacing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/4/1b4b92d67419c77c7a9bab9103274256eb1ba428.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^i$\" width=\"13\" height=\"15\" /> with the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/7/227cf962149eea38439581de276900288eb7ac46.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n-i)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"52\" height=\"18\" />-th heaviest weight in the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/3/5f32251987390debf7fc008d57f8015d67c3fe4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${2^{a_k}}$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/6/1467d53a41ba02613d3674a2f5654e9c23e2ea0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" />, and vice versa, forming a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/c/04c24ac2a2a3d8ab9f3f0f63c567f2a56164c670.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1:1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"32\" height=\"12\" /> relation. With this in mind, we use recursion upon the last weight placement. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f9a96c63183be91b9fe70e6e16dfa4bfec6f3da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" /> choices; namely, you can put any weight on either side except for the heaviest weight on the right. For the first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/d/4bdca93962a2beaffc172ca394e04fe05bd74ffd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> weight placements, the answer reduces to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/9/5a9d03ba669e8a27d65de50f5ee254ce729ac631.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W(n-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" />. We can reduce <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/9/5a9d03ba669e8a27d65de50f5ee254ce729ac631.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W(n-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" /> in the same way. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/7/477b50208b73370fcd4713e614b38d478be30efc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W(n)=(2n-1)W(n-1)=(2n-1)(2n-3)W(n-2)=...=(2n-1)!!W(1)=(2n-1)!!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"722\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/1/7a100311d7a1f32ae247c12a949fde4f9614b472.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{QED}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"36\" height=\"15\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose that we have a particular order of weights, and we are considering the number of ways we can place these. Note that if a weight is larger than all the preceding weights, than it must be placed into the left pan. Otherwise, it can be placed in either. To formalize this, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> is a permutation of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/9/7899362421a645cc48294571ebf30af7bbc73b47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 2, \\dots, n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" />, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/e/b5ecaecfedbce850acaadc20760ee8ad42c1932d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s(\\pi)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"36\" height=\"20\" /> denote the number of elements of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> that are smaller than at least one of the preceding elements. Then, we want <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/1/4b1ac786db8f55810db16b6a0cbb30e064f0d3cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum 2^{s(\\pi)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"65\" height=\"31\" />, running over all permutations of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/9/7899362421a645cc48294571ebf30af7bbc73b47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 2, \\dots, n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" />. Note that the probability that a given element <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> is counted in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/e/b5ecaecfedbce850acaadc20760ee8ad42c1932d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s(\\pi)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"36\" height=\"20\" /> for a random permutation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/c/d8c977a2d2587de026e4a7e4eef2a06de51aa555.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n-k}{n-k+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"80\" height=\"41\" />. Thus, the generating function for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/2/012834adaec7740a5bcd300430f378a3c47b67a1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(1+0x)(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x)(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}x)\\dots (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{n-1}{n}x) = \\prod_{k=1}^{n}(x+\\frac{1}{k}(1-x))\\]\" width=\"508\" height=\"48\" /> However, this is just a generating function for probabilities. We want to run it over all permutations, so we multiply by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />. Also, to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/6/406442706155cd172225934aa8c13f1c5fbec032.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{s(\\pi)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" />, transform <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/9/78942eb542771647e2eaf0ed9721dcb7b4169f88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\mapsto 2x$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"57\" height=\"12\" />. Thus, our final generating function is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/5/1656875f2d98d3afb91d56787c35dd13eff045b0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(n!)\\prod_{k=1}^{n}(2x+\\frac{1}{k}(1-2x))\\]\" width=\"189\" height=\"48\" /> To finish, we just want the sum of the coefficients, so we plug in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/5/4c52e02a35db152495396584bfeae1d0599cd753.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />: \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/9/4c9c07fed118ff5f30e85110e982fc4590579cb9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(n!)\\prod_{k=1}^{n}(2x+\\frac{1}{k}(1-2x)) = (n!)\\prod_{k=1}^{n}(2+\\frac{1}{k}(-1)) = \\prod_{k=1}^{n} 2k-1 = (2n-1)!!\\]\" width=\"536\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>-sillybone\n</p><p>-Reminded me of USAMTS 3/2/2026\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can compute the answer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/8/7987ffa8a557c433381a8250fa991b77298ce1b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> by conditioning on the position of the heaviest weight in the order of placement&#160;:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/0/75069917def4a8c700f6b9042ae8971cd7fad09d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[W(n)=\\sum_{k=1}^{n}W(k-1)2^{n-k}\\frac{(n-1)!}{(k-1)!}\\]\" width=\"271\" height=\"48\" />\nThe heaviest weight can only go to the left pan. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/1/271d619890c19e70139b14925c8156ec428631da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W(k-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"77\" height=\"20\" /> is the number of ways to place the first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/6/e16a82f688f3c61b4dcf0aded5468176eac1021c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(k-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" /> weights which got placed before the heaviest weight at position <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />. We used the fact that the number of valid ways does not depend on the actual weights because each weight is heavier than the sum of all the weights lighter than it. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/6/786cf63f01652e966247543294310b2568a0e746.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(n-1)!}{(k-1)!}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"67\" height=\"45\" /> ways to select <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/8/718f057bc155ad1e09dbdbf675cd30af26e7ba35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n-k)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" /> weights ( the order matters ) after the heaviest one out of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/5/7955459cf8b75c3ac12c23c393024177d7d56412.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" /> other weights . This is because there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/5/7955459cf8b75c3ac12c23c393024177d7d56412.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" /> ways to select the 1st weight after the heaviest, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/6/566aa684bcb55b4c157e26cb0a7faa94d391c3d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n-2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" /> to select the next one etc. \nEach of these <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/8/718f057bc155ad1e09dbdbf675cd30af26e7ba35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n-k)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" /> weights can go to the left or the right pan so there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/1/d210d26430ab59d70d2a6c06f4eb8a8a1e770fef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{n-k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"17\" /> ways to create all left-right combinations.  \nRearange as recursion:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/1/2017925365cd65ee006616401222b9b594d08eaa.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[W(n)=2(n-1)\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}W(k-1)2^{n-1-k}\\frac{(n-2)!}{(k-1)!}+W(n-1) = (2n-1)W(n-1)\\]\" width=\"573\" height=\"51\" />\nThat is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/2/a3248155c474cf609c6487850498620f91e79356.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[W(n)=(2n-1)(2n-3)...1 = (2n-1)!!\\]\" width=\"321\" height=\"18\" />\nas <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/8/898e0f40b05be50c7c52c33ea32ddbad60fd47a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W(1)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"18\" /> since if you have just one weight it can only go to the left pan.\n</p><p>--<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User:Alexander_skabelin&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"User:Alexander skabelin (page does not exist)\">alexander_skabelin</a> 9:24, 13 July 2023 (EST)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/2/2e23ccbe6aad8c6c9cd6182dfb6d9941b4a76ccb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Disclaimer}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"90\" height=\"13\" />: v_Enhance found this solution first here <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h418978p2365036\">[1]</a>.\n</p><p>If a sequence of placing the weights <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/9/33989028d1ea90a2e9858b7c01633dd069c4cc9c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^0,...,2^{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"85\" height=\"20\" /> satisfies the requirements call it a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/e/cbe4df19e04c1ee3147dfb5d5269a4db14d64c4d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textit{sequence}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"63\" height=\"12\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/d/04d5491ff2ea50c1d9f3a381d70bfe2b96f59304.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\chi^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"22\" height=\"18\" /> be the number of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-sequences. Suppose a non-empty subset of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/9/619648cf2f13d6addd7360baef058e93d9048a9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^k,2^{k+1},...,2^{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"128\" height=\"21\" /> is split into two piles, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/2/602172a66eee6b552713dfc2a219050b8822e217.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\leq k \\leq n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"108\" height=\"15\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/0/b10d5ee88ffc7d59f9c282b366953fb4fa4051f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" /> be the sum of the weights in the first pile and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/4/7f4caea56d945d0b6e49701b42ce06e0fb92e663.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" /> be the sum of the weights in the second pile. Note <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/2/64225beb5fce753f19aeca6b151c3f612a241686.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|S_1-S_2| \\geq 2^k \\geq 2 &gt; 2^0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"187\" height=\"22\" />. Thus every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/d/4bdca93962a2beaffc172ca394e04fe05bd74ffd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />-sequence induces (multiple) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-sequences by \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/0/9e0fbc754d4de8cefc5947ef135d09d281df12ef.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} (1):&amp; \\text{ Doubling the weights in the } n-1 \\text{-sequence} \\\\ (2):&amp; \\text{ Injecting } 2^0 \\text{ into the doubled } n-1 \\text{-sequence arbitrarily}  \\end{align*}\" width=\"444\" height=\"45\" />\nThere are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f9a96c63183be91b9fe70e6e16dfa4bfec6f3da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" /> ways to inject <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/f/43f697cd56b20fb242120d479824db503058069f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^0$\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" /> into the doubled <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/d/4bdca93962a2beaffc172ca394e04fe05bd74ffd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />-sequence arbitrarily (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/f/43f697cd56b20fb242120d479824db503058069f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^0$\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" /> can only go left if placed first, and may go left or right if not placed first). Every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-sequence is the result of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/9/0d9c3b1c142cbd3f8217dabc160c57e4750df241.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/f/62f62a19f0425d8e6121ca0c1cfd3ce96e43c9c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"18\" /> applied to some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/d/4bdca93962a2beaffc172ca394e04fe05bd74ffd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> sequence, and furthermore the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-sequences generated by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/9/0d9c3b1c142cbd3f8217dabc160c57e4750df241.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/f/62f62a19f0425d8e6121ca0c1cfd3ce96e43c9c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"18\" /> are unique. Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/5/5f5c68cf6e029f7420b8453d3702316d406eacdf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\chi^n = (2n-1) \\chi^{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"149\" height=\"21\" />. By induction\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/a/39af9fdf21560b468f2bfe2e1cdc3124821cd947.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\chi^n = (2n-1)(2n-3) \\cdots 3 \\cdot 1\\]\" width=\"232\" height=\"18\" />\nsince <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/2/2222e5deae58064403a026d04b150324eec379f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\chi^1 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"20\" />. \n</p><p>~not_detriti\n</p><p><i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2011_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_IMO\" title=\"2011 IMO\">2011 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_IMO_Problems\" title=\"2011 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2011 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2011 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2011 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2011 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2011 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2011 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2011 IMO Problems/Problem 